1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,896
Plug in your phone at 3 a.m.

2
00:00:02,846 --> 00:00:05,299
Then plug it into the same outlet

3
00:00:05,299 --> 00:00:07,753
at 3 p.m. Same wall, same wires.

4
00:00:08,274 --> 00:00:11,101
Depending on where you live, the power plants

5
00:00:11,101 --> 00:00:13,929
on the other end can be completely different.

6
00:00:13,879 --> 00:00:17,403
At night, maybe a wind farm on the plains.

7
00:00:17,403 --> 00:00:21,751
In the afternoon, solar panels that were dark at 3 a.m.

8
00:00:22,256 --> 00:00:25,754
Stick with me, and you'll see why, in sunny states, the

9
00:00:25,754 --> 00:00:28,935
toughest hour for the grid often isn't the hottest one.

10
00:00:28,885 --> 00:00:31,405
And why, some days, power plants

11
00:00:31,405 --> 00:00:33,925
pay to give their electricity away.

12
00:00:34,438 --> 00:00:37,447
Because the grid isn't one machine.

13
00:00:37,397 --> 00:00:40,335
It's a different machine every hour.

14
00:00:40,335 --> 00:00:43,779
So let's follow one day, hour by hour.

15
00:00:44,742 --> 00:00:47,059
Let's start in the dark.

16
00:00:47,009 --> 00:00:51,085
According to the Energy Information Administration, U.S. electricity use

17
00:00:51,085 --> 00:00:54,708
typically bottoms out around five in the morning.

18
00:00:54,708 --> 00:00:58,698
Most people are asleep, and most offices are closed.

19
00:00:59,204 --> 00:01:01,880
But it never drops to zero.

20
00:01:01,830 --> 00:01:06,471
Refrigerators and other devices that run all the time keep going.

21
00:01:06,471 --> 00:01:10,567
The EIA calls that steady floor of demand baseload.

22
00:01:11,074 --> 00:01:14,255
The classic baseload plant is nuclear.

23
00:01:14,205 --> 00:01:17,327
It's cheap to run once it's built, so operators try

24
00:01:17,327 --> 00:01:20,136
to keep it at full power around the clock.

25
00:01:20,640 --> 00:01:24,526
In 2025, the average U.S. nuclear plant ran at 91

26
00:01:24,526 --> 00:01:28,412
percent of its maximum possible output, higher than any other

27
00:01:28,412 --> 00:01:31,521
kind of power plant, according to the EIA.

28
00:01:31,471 --> 00:01:34,543
Nuclear supplied about 18 percent of

29
00:01:34,543 --> 00:01:37,614
the country's utility-scale electricity that year.

30
00:01:38,126 --> 00:01:40,310
Now add the wind.

31
00:01:40,260 --> 00:01:43,544
In the middle of the country, the EIA

32
00:01:43,544 --> 00:01:46,417
says land-based wind is usually strongest overnight.

33
00:01:46,417 --> 00:01:48,511
So wind records there tend to

34
00:01:48,511 --> 00:01:50,606
fall in the early morning hours.

35
00:01:51,124 --> 00:01:53,931
Take the Southwest Power Pool, the grid operator

36
00:01:53,931 --> 00:01:56,739
for a big stretch of the Great Plains.

37
00:01:56,689 --> 00:02:01,809
We ran its 2025 numbers from the EIA's hourly grid data.

38
00:02:01,809 --> 00:02:05,828
Averaged over the year, wind covered about 46 percent of demand

39
00:02:05,828 --> 00:02:09,481
at 2 a.m., but only about 31 percent at noon.

40
00:02:09,481 --> 00:02:13,032
And for five straight hours before dawn on

41
00:02:13,032 --> 00:02:16,583
December 28, 2025, it covered about 80 percent.

42
00:02:17,106 --> 00:02:20,040
In 2023, wind made up 36

43
00:02:20,040 --> 00:02:22,974
percent of that grid's total generation.

44
00:02:22,924 --> 00:02:25,592
Some nights there's more wind than anyone needs,

45
00:02:25,592 --> 00:02:28,261
so operators turn turbines down in the early

46
00:02:28,261 --> 00:02:30,596
morning to keep supply matched to demand.

47
00:02:31,562 --> 00:02:35,701
Twelve hours later, the machine looks completely different.

48
00:02:35,651 --> 00:02:39,398
The sun is high, air conditioners are running, and in a

49
00:02:39,398 --> 00:02:42,805
growing number of places, solar is doing the heavy lifting.

50
00:02:43,312 --> 00:02:46,001
Texas is a good example.

51
00:02:45,951 --> 00:02:49,994
In the summer of 2025, solar plants on the main

52
00:02:49,994 --> 00:02:54,036
Texas grid averaged 24 gigawatts between noon and one p.m.

53
00:02:54,036 --> 00:02:57,308
Two summers earlier, it was 12.

54
00:02:57,308 --> 00:02:59,834
That's double in two years.

55
00:03:00,342 --> 00:03:03,404
And natural gas made room for it.

56
00:03:03,354 --> 00:03:07,735
Gas supplied half of the Texas grid's midday power in the

57
00:03:07,735 --> 00:03:12,117
summer of 2023, and 37 percent in the summer of 2025.

58
00:03:12,620 --> 00:03:15,682
California has gone even further.

59
00:03:15,632 --> 00:03:18,825
According to the EIA, solar now supplies almost half

60
00:03:18,825 --> 00:03:22,018
of the California grid operator's demand between eight in

61
00:03:22,018 --> 00:03:24,501
the morning and four in the afternoon.

62
00:03:25,018 --> 00:03:29,599
And in the first five months of 2026, utility-scale solar generated

63
00:03:29,599 --> 00:03:34,181
more electricity than natural gas on 82 percent of days on

64
00:03:34,181 --> 00:03:38,346
that grid, up from 21 percent in 2024 and 2025.

65
00:03:38,808 --> 00:03:41,739
So at 3 a.m. on the Great Plains you might

66
00:03:41,739 --> 00:03:44,670
be running largely on wind, and at 3 p.m. in

67
00:03:44,670 --> 00:03:47,309
California, close to half of it might be sunshine.

68
00:03:47,259 --> 00:03:51,594
Same country, same kind of outlet, a different machine.

69
00:03:52,544 --> 00:03:55,380
Now here's the twist I promised.

70
00:03:55,330 --> 00:03:58,142
You'd think the toughest moment for the grid

71
00:03:58,142 --> 00:04:00,955
is the hottest afternoon, but often it isn't.

72
00:04:00,955 --> 00:04:05,423
In places with lots of solar, it's the stretch around sunset.

73
00:04:05,926 --> 00:04:10,357
To see why, grid operators look at something called net load.

74
00:04:10,307 --> 00:04:14,882
That's total demand minus whatever wind and solar are producing.

75
00:04:14,882 --> 00:04:18,539
It's the part every other power plant has to cover.

76
00:04:19,044 --> 00:04:21,510
Plot net load over a typical day in

77
00:04:21,510 --> 00:04:23,977
California and it sags deep in the middle,

78
00:04:23,977 --> 00:04:26,135
then shoots up steeply in the evening.

79
00:04:26,085 --> 00:04:28,226
It looks like the outline of a

80
00:04:28,226 --> 00:04:30,367
duck, so it's called the duck curve.

81
00:04:30,367 --> 00:04:32,681
And the EIA says the belly

82
00:04:32,681 --> 00:04:34,995
keeps getting deeper as solar grows.

83
00:04:35,498 --> 00:04:38,228
The belly has its own problem: in spring, when

84
00:04:38,228 --> 00:04:40,958
it's mild and demand is low, there can be

85
00:04:40,958 --> 00:04:43,081
more solar than the grid can use.

86
00:04:43,031 --> 00:04:48,350
In 2024, California's grid operator curtailed 3.4 million megawatt-hours of

87
00:04:48,350 --> 00:04:53,137
wind and solar, 29 percent more than in 2023.

88
00:04:53,137 --> 00:04:56,302
Solar was 93 percent of it.

89
00:04:56,824 --> 00:04:59,035
Then comes the neck.

90
00:04:58,985 --> 00:05:02,420
As solar fades around sunset, people get home, cook

91
00:05:02,420 --> 00:05:05,474
dinner and turn on lights and air conditioners.

92
00:05:05,474 --> 00:05:09,251
Everything that isn't the sun has to ramp up fast.

93
00:05:09,750 --> 00:05:12,519
So keep that steep neck in mind.

94
00:05:12,469 --> 00:05:14,876
Because the most interesting part of this story

95
00:05:14,876 --> 00:05:17,283
is what's being built to fill it, and

96
00:05:17,283 --> 00:05:19,690
what it does to the price of electricity.

97
00:05:20,654 --> 00:05:23,862
One fast-growing answer is a battery.

98
00:05:23,812 --> 00:05:26,182
Charge it with cheap midday solar, then

99
00:05:26,182 --> 00:05:28,214
let it run in the evening.

100
00:05:28,214 --> 00:05:30,938
The EIA puts it neatly: storing midday solar

101
00:05:30,938 --> 00:05:33,662
flattens the duck's curve, and sending it out

102
00:05:33,662 --> 00:05:36,046
in the evening shortens the duck's neck.

103
00:05:36,556 --> 00:05:39,232
The scale has changed fast.

104
00:05:39,182 --> 00:05:42,755
California's grid operator reported that in 2025, batteries

105
00:05:42,755 --> 00:05:46,327
in its market delivered about 9,100 megawatts on

106
00:05:46,327 --> 00:05:49,900
average in the hour ending at 8 p.m.

107
00:05:49,900 --> 00:05:54,980
In 2024, that figure was about 5,700.

108
00:05:55,482 --> 00:05:58,544
Texas is following the same pattern.

109
00:05:58,494 --> 00:06:01,946
In the summer of 2025, batteries on its main grid

110
00:06:01,946 --> 00:06:05,399
supplied an average of 4 gigawatts in the 8 p.m.

111
00:06:05,399 --> 00:06:08,161
hour, right as the solar output was fading.

112
00:06:08,672 --> 00:06:11,734
Batteries don't make energy, though.

113
00:06:11,684 --> 00:06:15,115
They shift it, and they lose some along the way.

114
00:06:15,115 --> 00:06:19,051
In 2019, the EIA found the U.S. battery fleet gave

115
00:06:19,051 --> 00:06:22,987
back about 82 percent of the electricity put into it.

116
00:06:23,494 --> 00:06:26,941
And operators run them largely on price.

117
00:06:26,891 --> 00:06:30,648
In 2024, 41 percent of U.S. utility-scale

118
00:06:30,648 --> 00:06:34,405
battery capacity was used mainly for arbitrage.

119
00:06:34,405 --> 00:06:37,347
That's a fancy word for charging when power is

120
00:06:37,347 --> 00:06:39,963
cheap and selling it back when it's expensive.

121
00:06:40,926 --> 00:06:43,363
Which brings us to price.

122
00:06:43,313 --> 00:06:46,070
On the wholesale market, where power is bought

123
00:06:46,070 --> 00:06:48,827
and sold in bulk, electricity can cost wildly

124
00:06:48,827 --> 00:06:51,584
different amounts from one hour to the next.

125
00:06:52,100 --> 00:06:56,783
Berkeley Lab found that the average wholesale price across the major

126
00:06:56,783 --> 00:07:01,039
U.S. power markets in 2023 was 36 dollars per megawatt-hour.

127
00:07:00,989 --> 00:07:04,074
But that average hides huge swings.

128
00:07:04,074 --> 00:07:07,146
That year in Texas, hourly prices on

129
00:07:07,146 --> 00:07:09,779
some summer evenings topped 500 dollars.

130
00:07:09,779 --> 00:07:14,473
In California, on sunny spring days, they dropped below zero.

131
00:07:14,986 --> 00:07:16,904
Below zero.

132
00:07:16,854 --> 00:07:20,524
That's the strange part I promised at the start.

133
00:07:20,524 --> 00:07:22,772
A negative price means sellers are

134
00:07:22,772 --> 00:07:25,019
paying buyers to take their power.

135
00:07:25,536 --> 00:07:27,920
Why would anyone do that?

136
00:07:27,870 --> 00:07:30,702
The EIA explains that some plants can't easily

137
00:07:30,702 --> 00:07:33,534
or cheaply shut down for a short stretch.

138
00:07:33,534 --> 00:07:37,105
Nuclear operators try to run at full power, and big

139
00:07:37,105 --> 00:07:40,675
steam plants face costs every time they stop and restart.

140
00:07:41,198 --> 00:07:44,139
And some wind farms earn a tax credit for

141
00:07:44,139 --> 00:07:47,079
every megawatt-hour they produce, so they may keep running

142
00:07:47,079 --> 00:07:49,366
even when the price dips below zero.

143
00:07:49,316 --> 00:07:53,279
For a short stretch, paying a little beats shutting down.

144
00:07:53,788 --> 00:07:57,203
In California's day-ahead market, prices were negative about

145
00:07:57,203 --> 00:08:00,617
7 percent of the time in 2025, according

146
00:08:00,617 --> 00:08:03,605
to the grid operator's own market monitor.

147
00:08:03,555 --> 00:08:06,742
And Berkeley Lab found that in 2023, California's

148
00:08:06,742 --> 00:08:09,929
negative prices showed up almost entirely in sunny

149
00:08:09,929 --> 00:08:12,717
hours of late spring and early summer.

150
00:08:13,218 --> 00:08:15,686
On the other end are the expensive

151
00:08:15,686 --> 00:08:18,155
hours, when the grid calls on peakers.

152
00:08:18,105 --> 00:08:22,174
These are often gas turbines that run only occasionally.

153
00:08:22,174 --> 00:08:25,356
They cost more to run, but they respond quickly,

154
00:08:25,356 --> 00:08:28,184
which is exactly what that evening ramp needs.

155
00:08:28,688 --> 00:08:31,856
Someone has to coordinate all of this.

156
00:08:31,806 --> 00:08:35,589
That job belongs to balancing authorities, dozens of them across

157
00:08:35,589 --> 00:08:39,373
the country, keeping supply and demand matched in real time.

158
00:08:39,886 --> 00:08:43,791
If supply and demand drift too far apart, the EIA

159
00:08:43,791 --> 00:08:47,695
warns, the result can be local or even wide-area blackouts.

160
00:08:47,645 --> 00:08:51,847
In the big regional markets, prices help with that job.

161
00:08:51,847 --> 00:08:54,670
The EIA notes they tend to fall when solar

162
00:08:54,670 --> 00:08:57,180
output is high and rise as it fades.

163
00:08:57,684 --> 00:09:00,360
And you can watch it yourself.

164
00:09:00,310 --> 00:09:04,005
The EIA's free Hourly Electric Grid Monitor shows demand and

165
00:09:04,005 --> 00:09:07,331
the mix of sources by region, hour by hour.

166
00:09:08,300 --> 00:09:11,136
So what does this mean at your house?

167
00:09:11,086 --> 00:09:14,410
Most of us never see wholesale prices.

168
00:09:14,410 --> 00:09:17,110
But your timing still matters.

169
00:09:17,626 --> 00:09:20,726
Most electric vehicle drivers charge overnight at

170
00:09:20,726 --> 00:09:23,826
home, according to the Department of Energy.

171
00:09:23,776 --> 00:09:27,465
And some utilities offer time-of-use rates, where electricity costs

172
00:09:27,465 --> 00:09:30,744
less in certain hours and more in others.

173
00:09:31,248 --> 00:09:34,243
That's possible because most homes now have smart

174
00:09:34,243 --> 00:09:36,863
meters that record use at least hourly.

175
00:09:36,813 --> 00:09:41,042
In 2022, about 73 percent of U.S. residential

176
00:09:41,042 --> 00:09:45,270
electricity meters were smart meters, the EIA reports.

177
00:09:45,782 --> 00:09:48,923
And here's where the old advice breaks down.

178
00:09:48,873 --> 00:09:51,385
On California's wholesale market, some of the cheapest

179
00:09:51,385 --> 00:09:53,897
hours are now in the middle of the

180
00:09:53,897 --> 00:09:56,094
day, not the middle of the night.

181
00:09:56,094 --> 00:10:00,084
So read your own utility's rate plan instead of guessing.

182
00:10:00,604 --> 00:10:03,232
Running the dishwasher or charging the car in

183
00:10:03,232 --> 00:10:05,860
a cheap hour isn't just about your bill.

184
00:10:05,810 --> 00:10:08,123
It's you helping the grid fill the

185
00:10:08,123 --> 00:10:10,105
duck's belly instead of its neck.

186
00:10:11,076 --> 00:10:12,981
Quick recap.

187
00:10:12,931 --> 00:10:15,604
At 3 a.m., demand is near its low,

188
00:10:15,604 --> 00:10:18,278
nuclear runs flat out, and on the Great

189
00:10:18,278 --> 00:10:20,617
Plains, wind is often at its strongest.

190
00:10:21,122 --> 00:10:24,497
At 3 p.m., solar takes over more and more of

191
00:10:24,497 --> 00:10:27,535
the load, sometimes so much that prices go negative.

192
00:10:27,485 --> 00:10:30,648
Then at sunset comes the steep neck of the duck,

193
00:10:30,648 --> 00:10:33,495
and batteries and gas plants race to cover it.

194
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,663
The grid isn't one machine.

195
00:10:36,613 --> 00:10:39,552
It's a different machine every hour.

196
00:10:39,552 --> 00:10:42,158
And now you know how to read it.

197
00:10:42,678 --> 00:10:45,372
Now I'm curious: what does your utility

198
00:10:45,372 --> 00:10:48,067
charge at night versus in the afternoon?

199
00:10:48,017 --> 00:10:51,860
Do you have a time-of-use rate, or one flat price?

200
00:10:51,860 --> 00:10:54,652
Tell me your state in the comments.

201
00:10:55,172 --> 00:10:58,285
If you like seeing how the hidden machinery of

202
00:10:58,285 --> 00:11:01,399
everyday life really works, subscribe to The Hidden Grid.

203
00:11:01,349 --> 00:11:03,809
And every source behind the numbers in

204
00:11:03,809 --> 00:11:06,269
this video is listed in the description.
